call_details.response:
The board contains 13 pieces with no immediate Connect-4 available for either player (verified by enumerating every landing square and every line of length 4 through those squares). Player o therefore looks for the move that simultaneously claims the largest number of still-open winning windows and denies the opponent the same windows.

Column 3 is the unique file whose next square (row 2) lies on every horizontal 4-in-a-row of that rank. Occupying it:
- places an o in all four horizontal windows of row 2, keeping them alive for o while immediately killing all of them for x;
- converts the diagonal window {(0,5),(1,4),(2,3),(3,2)} into a 2-o threat;
- blocks the two dangerous x-diagonals that run through (1,2)–(2,3).

No other file produces a comparable gain. In particular:
- Column 1 activates o’s existing diagonal 3-in-a-row, but x simply occupies the hole and the net exchange is inferior;
- Columns 0, 4, 5 and 6 each create at most a single 2-in-a-row and leave the vital row-2 centre free for x.

Thus column 3 is the unique optimal continuation.

Final Answer: 3

